<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    MIASMA_MECHANICS_1 M6 build pass. The DutyDef RM_CompTerritorialAnchor's
    own header comment already sketches by name (<dutyDef>RM_AnchorGuard</dutyDef>)
    built here so the roster pass has a ready-made duty to reference,
    generic across any future anchored creature (Sump's own S3 tar beast
    included), not Miasma-specific.

    ThinkNode_Priority tries IN ORDER, same convention as this repo's own
    Duties_Inhabited.xml precedent: fight anything hostile-or-harvesting
    inside the anchor radius first, else wander near the anchor. Both
    JobGivers are this mod's own generalization of vanilla's hive-defense
    pair (RM_CompTerritorialAnchor.cs's own header: RimWorld/
    JobGiver_HiveDefense.cs / JobGiver_WanderHive.cs, confirmed against the
    live 1.6 decompile at that file's own spike pass), reading
    pawn.mindState.duty.focus/radius for whatever Thing
    RM_CompTerritorialAnchor.SetAnchor() was given, not a hardcoded Hive.

    🔴 CORRECTED by WARDEN_MOTHER_BEFRIENDING_1 (this file's own comment was
    accurate but the gap it named is now closed, not merely flagged): a
    plain vanilla Animal ThinkTreeDef genuinely never consults
    mindState.duty on its own, so this DutyDef's own <thinkNode> above is
    never reached by dispatch through a pawn's assigned ThinkTreeDef.
    RM_WardenMother's PawnKindDef does NOT need — and does not use — an
    Insect-shaped ThinkTreeDef to make this work. Instead
    RM_JobGiver_AnchorDefense / RM_JobGiver_AnchorWander are inserted
    directly, GLOBALLY, via RM_ThinkTree_AnchorBehaviors's
    insertTag="Animal_PreMain" (Defs/ThinkTreeDefs/, same folder), which
    runs for every animal regardless of its own ThinkTreeDef — the two
    JobGivers still read pawn.mindState.duty.focus/radius exactly as this
    DutyDef sets it via RM_CompTerritorialAnchor.SetAnchor(), just reached
    by a different dispatch path than the <thinkNode> below. This DutyDef
    stays as the PawnDuty.def marker RM_CompTerritorialAnchor.ApplyDuty()
    constructs a PawnDuty from; its own <thinkNode> XML is now inert
    (nothing ever runs it) and is kept only because removing the DutyDef
    entirely would break that constructor call.
  -->
  <DutyDef>
    <defName>RM_AnchorGuard</defName>
    <thinkNode Class="ThinkNode_Priority">
      <subNodes>

        <li Class="RimMandrake.EnvironmentalHazards.RM_JobGiver_AnchorDefense" />

        <li Class="RimMandrake.EnvironmentalHazards.RM_JobGiver_AnchorWander" />

      </subNodes>
    </thinkNode>
  </DutyDef>

</Defs>
